er on kind of a low speed  slowly drizzle it in so that it has time  to fully mix in to the butter and sugar  great now the egg and the butter and the  sugar are all mixed in so it's not going  to be quite as fluffy because we've  added a little bit of egg but it should  be kind of creamy looking and you don't  want pieces of egg mixed in with pieces  of butter you really want it to be kind  of homogeneous because if it's separated  then when the dry ingredients hit it  then the dry ingredients are going to  latch onto the egg and they're going to  latch onto the butter and you're going  to end up with weird little pockets I'm  going to give it another scrape  make sure we get all of that  and this is looking really beautiful now  I did want to show you you can see like  when I cracked the egg there was a  little bit of eggshell so this is why we  crack into the bowl and then pour the  eggs because if this ends up in  somebody's cookie have you ever had a  cookie with eggshell it's like you'd  think it wouldn't be so bad you'd think  it'd be nice and crunchy but it's pretty  nasty great let's now mix our chocolate  in with the flour so the reason I mix  the chocolate into the flour is that  that way I can combine the flour into  the egg sugar and butter mixture with  minimal gluten development because  remember the gluten will start  developing as soon as the flour hits  anything liquid  foreign  I like to mix this part with the mixer  and then I like to finish it by hand  because I don't want to over mix if I  were to turn this on all the flour would  go everywhere so I'm just going to pulse  on off on off  so I've mixed it pretty much all the way  but I'm actually going to finish mixing  it by hand I like to finally feel the  dough I've let the machine do everything  and now I want to do something  so let's go ahead and take this out  and then also it allows me to control  how much how much mixing we're doing so  that we don't overwork the dough  butter and sugar 